      title: "Confucius and the Analects",
      body: `## The life

**Kong Qiu**, known to his followers as Kongzi, "Master Kong," and in the West by the Latinized name **Confucius** given him by Jesuit missionaries in the seventeenth century, was born in 551 BC in the small state of **Lu**, in what is now Shandong. His family belonged to the lower ranks of the aristocracy, and he grew up poor. He held minor offices—according to tradition, as a keeper of granaries and of livestock—and may briefly have held a higher post in Lu, though later accounts greatly exaggerate his political career. Disappointed in Lu, he spent years traveling among the states with his disciples, seeking a ruler who would put his ideas into practice. He found none. He returned to Lu in old age, taught, and died in 479 BC, regarding himself as a failure.

![A standing figure of Confucius in long robes with his hands clasped before him.](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Confucius_Tang_Dynasty.jpg "Confucius, after a portrait traditionally attributed to the Tang painter Wu Daozi (8th century). No likeness made in his lifetime survives.")

## The Analects

Confucius wrote nothing that survives. What we know of his teaching comes chiefly from the **Analects** (*Lunyu*, "Selected Sayings"), a collection of short sayings and conversations compiled by his disciples and their followers over several generations after his death. The Analects is not a systematic treatise. It is a collection of brief, often enigmatic exchanges—"The Master said..."—arranged in twenty books with no obvious order. Scholars believe some books are considerably later than others. Yet it presents a vivid portrait of a teacher: demanding, humorous, sometimes impatient, devoted to learning, and grieving bitterly at the death of his favorite disciple, **Yan Hui**.

Confucius described himself as "a transmitter and not a maker, believing in and loving the ancients." He saw his mission as the recovery of the way of the early Zhou and the Duke of Zhou. But in recovering the past, he transformed it.

## The gentleman

Central to Confucius's teaching is the figure of the **junzi**. The word originally meant "son of a lord," a nobleman by birth. Confucius made it mean a nobleman by character: a person of moral cultivation, whatever his birth. Translators render it "gentleman," "exemplary person," or "superior man." The junzi is contrasted with the *xiaoren*, the "small man" or petty person: "The gentleman understands what is right; the small man understands what is profitable." To become a junzi was a lifelong task of learning and self-cultivation. Confucius described his own progress: "At fifteen, I had my mind bent on learning. At thirty, I stood firm. At forty, I had no doubts. At fifty, I knew the decrees of Heaven. At sixty, my ear was an obedient organ for the reception of truth. At seventy, I could follow what my heart desired, without transgressing what was right."

## Ren and li

Two concepts lie at the center of Confucius's ethics. The first is **ren**, variously translated "benevolence," "humaneness," "goodness," or "authoritative conduct." It is the supreme virtue, the quality of a fully realized human being in relation to others. Confucius was reluctant to define it and rarely credited anyone, including himself, with achieving it. Asked by a disciple for a single word that could guide one's whole life, he answered with a related concept, *shu*, "reciprocity": "What you do not want done to yourself, do not do to others."

The second is **li**, "ritual" or "propriety." Originally the term referred to the sacrificial rites of the Zhou aristocracy. Confucius extended it to the whole range of conventional behavior—ceremonies, manners, forms of address, the proper conduct of sons toward fathers, ministers toward rulers, and friends toward friends. Li was not empty formality. Confucius insisted that ritual without feeling was worthless: "If a man be without the virtues proper to humanity, what has he to do with the rites of propriety?" But he also believed that the practice of ritual shaped feeling, training people in respect and restraint, and that a society governed by ritual would be harmonious without coercion.

## Family and state

For Confucius, morality began in the family. **Filial piety** (*xiao*)—reverence and obedience toward one's parents, and care for them in old age and sacrifices to them after death—was the root of all virtue. A person who learned to respect his parents and elder brothers would naturally respect his ruler. Confucius praised a son who concealed his father's crime, against a provincial governor who boasted of a son who had testified against his father for stealing a sheep: "Among us, in our part of the country, those who are upright are different from this. The father conceals the misconduct of the son, and the son conceals the misconduct of the father. Uprightness is to be found in this."

## Government by virtue

Confucius's political teaching followed from his ethics. The ruler should govern by moral example, not by force: "He who exercises government by means of his virtue may be compared to the north polar star, which keeps its place and all the stars turn towards it." Laws and punishments might make the people obey, but they would not make them good; ritual and virtue would. Asked what he would do first if he were given charge of a government, Confucius replied that he would "rectify names"—make sure that words corresponded to realities, so that a ruler was truly a ruler, a minister truly a minister, a father truly a father, and a son truly a son.

Confucius's teaching was, in one sense, conservative: it looked to the past and defended hierarchy. But it was also radical. It claimed that moral worth, not birth, should determine who governed; that rulers were bound by moral standards; and that education could make anyone a gentleman. Confucius accepted students of any background who brought him a token gift, "from the man bringing his bundle of dried flesh for my teaching upwards." That idea—that the right to rule belonged to the educated and virtuous—would eventually reshape Chinese government.

> **Pause and consider.** Confucius praised the son who concealed his father's theft. What does this passage reveal about the relation between family loyalty and public justice in his thought? How would you answer him?`,
